\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Symmetry reduction cascade for the planar three-body problem. Each step quotients a symmetry and removes degrees of freedom: translation (COM frame), rotation ($\tilde  \rho $ to $+x$), reflection ($\tilde  \lambda _y\ge 0$), and scale ($I=1$). The final 5-DOF reduced space (2 mass + 2 shape + 1 momentum orientation) is what the decoder parameterises.}}{16}{figure.0.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:symmetry_cascade}{{1}{16}{Symmetry reduction cascade for the planar three-body problem. Each step quotients a symmetry and removes degrees of freedom: translation (COM frame), rotation ($\tilde \rho $ to $+x$), reflection ($\tilde \lambda _y\ge 0$), and scale ($I=1$). The final 5-DOF reduced space (2 mass + 2 shape + 1 momentum orientation) is what the decoder parameterises}{figure.0.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Injectivity caveats. Left: the canonical frame seam where $\tilde  \lambda _y=0$, with the deadband $\delta _\lambda $ stabilising the mirror rule. Centre: degenerate and collision-at-$t=0$ configurations are tagged with terminal labels, not rejected---every pixel gets a well-defined output. Right: the no-holes guarantee as a branching flow: every UV pixel either produces a valid IC for simulation or receives a labelled terminal tag.}}{17}{figure.0.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:injectivity}{{2}{17}{Injectivity caveats. Left: the canonical frame seam where $\tilde \lambda _y=0$, with the deadband $\delta _\lambda $ stabilising the mirror rule. Centre: degenerate and collision-at-$t=0$ configurations are tagged with terminal labels, not rejected---every pixel gets a well-defined output. Right: the no-holes guarantee as a branching flow: every UV pixel either produces a valid IC for simulation or receives a labelled terminal tag}{figure.0.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The chart atlas. Four different coordinate frames drawn on the same manifold $\mathcal  {M}$ (shown inside a perspective wireframe cube representing the ambient space). Each pair of basis vectors---red $(q_1, q_2)$ for affine slicing, blue $(L_z, E)$ for invariant momentum, green $(\theta , \varphi )$ for the shape sphere, orange $(\nu , K)$ for the Burrau family---defines a 2D chart through $\mathcal  {M}$ at a different orientation. All charts feed the same shared decoder $D\circ C$; the GPU shader is chart-agnostic.}}{18}{figure.0.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:chart_atlas}{{3}{18}{The chart atlas. Four different coordinate frames drawn on the same manifold $\mathcal {M}$ (shown inside a perspective wireframe cube representing the ambient space). Each pair of basis vectors---red $(q_1, q_2)$ for affine slicing, blue $(L_z, E)$ for invariant momentum, green $(\theta , \varphi )$ for the shape sphere, orange $(\nu , K)$ for the Burrau family---defines a 2D chart through $\mathcal {M}$ at a different orientation. All charts feed the same shared decoder $D\circ C$; the GPU shader is chart-agnostic}{figure.0.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Slicing the manifold. Left: a 2D viewing plane (blue parallelogram) defined by basis vectors $q_1$ (red) and $q_2$ (blue) cuts through the manifold $\mathcal  {M}$ at the lock point $z_0$. The dashed arrow indicates hidden dimensions that the tilt gesture rotates into. Right: the resulting $[0,1]^2$ cross-section as rendered---each pixel is one simulated trajectory, coloured by outcome (blue: escape 1--2, red: escape 2--3, green: bounded). The fractal basin boundary is visible where the basins meet. Changing the slice orientation (tilt) reveals different cross-sections of the same high-dimensional structure.}}{19}{figure.0.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:manifold_projections}{{4}{19}{Slicing the manifold. Left: a 2D viewing plane (blue parallelogram) defined by basis vectors $q_1$ (red) and $q_2$ (blue) cuts through the manifold $\mathcal {M}$ at the lock point $z_0$. The dashed arrow indicates hidden dimensions that the tilt gesture rotates into. Right: the resulting $[0,1]^2$ cross-section as rendered---each pixel is one simulated trajectory, coloured by outcome (blue: escape 1--2, red: escape 2--3, green: bounded). The fractal basin boundary is visible where the basins meet. Changing the slice orientation (tilt) reveals different cross-sections of the same high-dimensional structure}{figure.0.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The decode pipeline. Chart coordinates in $[0,1]^2$ are mapped through the chart map $\Phi $, decoder $D$ (factorised as mass $\times $ configuration $\times $ momentum), and canonicaliser $C$ to produce a physical IC. Degenerate configurations are tagged rather than rejected.}}{20}{figure.0.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:decode_pipeline}{{5}{20}{The decode pipeline. Chart coordinates in $[0,1]^2$ are mapped through the chart map $\Phi $, decoder $D$ (factorised as mass $\times $ configuration $\times $ momentum), and canonicaliser $C$ to produce a physical IC. Degenerate configurations are tagged rather than rejected}{figure.0.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The IC manifold factorisation. The decode parameter space $Y$ decomposes as mass (2\nobreakspace  {}DOF) $\times $ configuration (2\nobreakspace  {}DOF, after rotation and scale gauges are fixed) $\times $ momentum (4\nobreakspace  {}DOF) = 8\nobreakspace  {}DOF total. Each subspace has its own decode stage; the join assembles the physical IC.}}{21}{figure.0.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:factorisation}{{6}{21}{The IC manifold factorisation. The decode parameter space $Y$ decomposes as mass (2~DOF) $\times $ configuration (2~DOF, after rotation and scale gauges are fixed) $\times $ momentum (4~DOF) = 8~DOF total. Each subspace has its own decode stage; the join assembles the physical IC}{figure.0.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Hyperspherical mass-weighted Jacobi coordinates. Left: the physical Jacobi vectors $\bm  {\rho }$ (inner pair, red) and $\bm  {\lambda }$ (outer body relative to inner COM, blue) drawn on an actual three-body triangle. Centre: the canonical-frame parameterisation with $\alpha $ controlling the $|\tilde  \rho |/|\tilde  \lambda |$ ratio, $\beta \in [0,\pi ]$ the angle between them, and $I=1$ as the scale gauge. Right: how increasing $\alpha $ transforms the triangle from comparable inner/outer scales (compact, $\alpha \to 0$ gives $\|\tilde  \rho \|=1$, $\|\tilde  \lambda \|=0$, body\nobreakspace  {}2 sitting at the inner-pair COM) toward a hierarchical configuration with a tight inner pair and a distant third body ($\alpha \to \pi /2$ gives $\|\tilde  \rho \|\to 0$, $\|\tilde  \lambda \|=1$).}}{22}{figure.0.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hyperspherical_jacobi}{{7}{22}{Hyperspherical mass-weighted Jacobi coordinates. Left: the physical Jacobi vectors $\boldsymbol \rho $ (inner pair, red) and $\boldsymbol \lambda $ (outer body relative to inner COM, blue) drawn on an actual three-body triangle. Centre: the canonical-frame parameterisation with $\alpha $ controlling the $|\tilde \rho |/|\tilde \lambda |$ ratio, $\beta \in [0,\pi ]$ the angle between them, and $I=1$ as the scale gauge. Right: how increasing $\alpha $ transforms the triangle from comparable inner/outer scales (compact, $\alpha \to 0$ gives $\|\tilde \rho \|=1$, $\|\tilde \lambda \|=0$, body~2 sitting at the inner-pair COM) toward a hierarchical configuration with a tight inner pair and a distant third body ($\alpha \to \pi /2$ gives $\|\tilde \rho \|\to 0$, $\|\tilde \lambda \|=1$)}{figure.0.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The shape sphere $S^2$ coloured by the Jacobi angle $\alpha $. Note that with the canonical-frame decode (\S  \ref  {sec:hyperspherical_jacobi}) $\|\tilde  \rho \|=\qopname  \relax o{cos}\alpha $ and $\|\tilde  \lambda \|=\qopname  \relax o{sin}\alpha $, so small $\alpha $ corresponds to a \emph  {large} inner-pair separation, not a tight one. Red: $\alpha \approx 0$ (body\nobreakspace  {}2 near the inner-pair COM, $\|\tilde  \lambda \|\to 0$). Blue: $\alpha \approx \pi /2$ (tight inner pair, hierarchical with a distant third body). White at the equator: $\alpha =\pi /4$, equal $\|\tilde  \rho \|$ and $\|\tilde  \lambda \|$. Right panel: example triangles at six $\alpha $ values, colour-matched to their position on the sphere.}}{23}{figure.0.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:alpha_sphere}{{8}{23}{The shape sphere $S^2$ coloured by the Jacobi angle $\alpha $. Note that with the canonical-frame decode (\S \ref {sec:hyperspherical_jacobi}) $\|\tilde \rho \|=\cos \alpha $ and $\|\tilde \lambda \|=\sin \alpha $, so small $\alpha $ corresponds to a \emph {large} inner-pair separation, not a tight one. Red: $\alpha \approx 0$ (body~2 near the inner-pair COM, $\|\tilde \lambda \|\to 0$). Blue: $\alpha \approx \pi /2$ (tight inner pair, hierarchical with a distant third body). White at the equator: $\alpha =\pi /4$, equal $\|\tilde \rho \|$ and $\|\tilde \lambda \|$. Right panel: example triangles at six $\alpha $ values, colour-matched to their position on the sphere}{figure.0.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The $(L_z,E)$ feasibility region. The parabolic boundary $|L_z|\le \sqrt  {2I(E-U)}$ separates the feasible region (green, $K\ge 0$) from the infeasible region (red, $K<0$). The rest-start point $(0,U)$ sits at the parabola's apex. The domain warp maps the $[0,1]^2$ UV square into the feasible interior, guaranteeing every pixel produces a valid IC.}}{24}{figure.0.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:feasibility_parabola}{{9}{24}{The $(L_z,E)$ feasibility region. The parabolic boundary $|L_z|\le \sqrt {2I(E-U)}$ separates the feasible region (green, $K\ge 0$) from the infeasible region (red, $K<0$). The rest-start point $(0,U)$ sits at the parabola's apex. The domain warp maps the $[0,1]^2$ UV square into the feasible interior, guaranteeing every pixel produces a valid IC}{figure.0.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The shape sphere $S^2$ with all distinguished configurations labelled (equal-mass conventions). Red circles: binary collision singularities BC$_{12}$, BC$_{23}$, BC$_{31}$ on the equator at $120^\circ $ intervals. Orange diamonds: Euler collinear configurations $E_1$, $E_2$, $E_3$ (antipodal to BCs). Green triangles: Lagrange equilateral poles $L^+$ (north), $L^-$ (south). Dashed purple arcs: branch cuts $C_a$, $C_b$ for the free-group word encoding.}}{24}{figure.0.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:labelled_sphere}{{10}{24}{The shape sphere $S^2$ with all distinguished configurations labelled (equal-mass conventions). Red circles: binary collision singularities BC$_{12}$, BC$_{23}$, BC$_{31}$ on the equator at $120^\circ $ intervals. Orange diamonds: Euler collinear configurations $E_1$, $E_2$, $E_3$ (antipodal to BCs). Green triangles: Lagrange equilateral poles $L^+$ (north), $L^-$ (south). Dashed purple arcs: branch cuts $C_a$, $C_b$ for the free-group word encoding}{figure.0.10}{}}
